---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
    title="How it works"
    current="how"
    description="A sport, a loop, and a course file for your watch or GPS, or several sports in one plan."
>
    <main class="wrap page">
        <p class="kicker">How it works</p>
        <h1>The line comes from the map, not from a guess.</h1>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                You set a start pin, a sport, a distance, a surface, and an
                elevation bias. The sport can be a run, a walk, a hike, or a
                ride. Traceway asks a routing engine for several loops and ranks
                them. Distance error, surface, climb, quiet streets, trunk
                roads, and ferries decide the order.
            </p>
            <p>
                Unknown surface stays unknown. A sentence can fill the same
                filters later. It does not invent coordinates.
            </p>
            <h2>Several sports in one plan</h2>
            <p>
                A multisport plan is a row of legs that meet at a transition.
                Each leg on land is its own route, drawn by the same engine with
                its own sport, length, surface, and hills. The swim is the
                exception. A routing engine cannot route on water, so you draw
                it point by point, and Traceway keeps it as you drew it.
            </p>
            <p>
                The legs can all leave the transition and come back to it, or
                they can chain, each ending where the next begins. A chained leg
                takes the shortest way to its handover, or winds out to the
                length you set. Either way, no leg turns back on its own road,
                and none runs back over a road an earlier leg used.
            </p>
            <h2>Before you go</h2>
            <p>
                Every route has a player under the map. Slide it from the start
                to the finish, or press play, and a pin follows the line. The
                map keeps the pin in view, beside the plan and not under it.
            </p>
            <p>
                The planner itself lives in the app. This site is the quiet
                explanation you can read before you open it.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="page-cta">
            <a class="fill" href="/dashboard">Open the planner</a>
            <small>Loops for every sport.</small>
        </div>
    </main>
</Base>
